---
/**
 * A code block. `@sanity/code-input`'s type, rendered as `<pre><code>`.
 *
 * ── IT WAS CALLED `pre`, AND THAT WAS WRONG ─────────────────────────────────
 *
 * Renamed 2026-08-26, data and all. The schema declared the array member as
 * `{name: 'pre', title: 'Pre', type: 'code'}`, and Sanity stores an array member's
 * NAME as its `_type` — so the stored blocks said `pre`.
 *
 * `pre` named the HTML element this renders to rather than the thing it is, which
 * is a presentational leak into the content model, and it named it inaccurately
 * anyway: the output is `<pre><code>`. The fields are `code`, `language`,
 * `filename` and `highlightedLines`. It is a code block.
 *
 * THIS ALSO RETIRES A FINDING. The mismatch was reported here as TypeGen
 * describing the schema rather than the data — TypeGen said `code` where the data
 * said `pre`. TypeGen had it right: `code` was always the type, and the `name`
 * override was the inconsistency. Data, schema and generated types now agree.
 *
 * The 25 existing blocks were migrated by setting `_type` on each keyed path, so
 * the `code` payloads were never rewritten — 5715 characters before, 5715 after.
 *
 * ── THE WHITESPACE TRAP, INVERTED ────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * Phase 3 recorded that Astro TRIMS whitespace before an element on a new line,
 * which is why `{' '}` appears throughout the masthead. Inside `<pre>` the same
 * mechanism bites the other way round: `<pre>` preserves whitespace, so any
 * newline and indent Astro leaves between the tags becomes a blank first line and
 * a leading indent on every subsequent one.
 *
 *     <pre>              ->  renders a blank line, then the code indented
 *       <code>{code}</code>
 *     </pre>
 *
 * ── AND WHY THE ELEMENT BELOW IS SELF-CLOSING ────────────────────────────────
 *
 * Writing it on one line does not survive: Prettier reformats the template on
 * every `pnpm format`, and it broke exactly this element across three lines,
 * putting `\n      ` inside the `<pre>` in the built HTML. `<!-- prettier-ignore -->`
 * does not help either — it applies to elements, not to `{…}` expression blocks,
 * and Prettier collapsed the comment onto the line and reformatted regardless.
 * Both were tried and both were verified in dist/, not assumed.
 *
 * So the fix is structural rather than a convention anyone has to hold to: an
 * element with NO CHILDREN has nothing for a formatter to break. `set:html` puts
 * the `<code>` wrapper inside the attribute, where whitespace cannot be
 * introduced, and the block is immune to reformatting by construction.
 *
 * ── `tabindex="0"` IS NOT DECORATION ─────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * base.css gives the block `overflow-x: auto`, because the longest line in the
 * content is 117 characters against a 656px column. A scrollable region that
 * cannot be reached by keyboard fails WCAG 2.1.1 — a pointer user can drag it and
 * a keyboard user cannot reach it at all. Making the `<pre>` focusable is the
 * standard remedy and lets arrow keys scroll it.
 *
 * No `role="region"` and no `aria-label` to go with it. Those would make every
 * code block a landmark, and 21 of them in one article would bury the page's real
 * landmarks in noise. A focusable `<pre>` announces its own contents.
 *
 * ── TWO FIELDS DELIBERATELY UNRENDERED ───────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * `filename` and `highlightedLines` are on the type and set on NO block in the
 * dataset. Rendering a filename header or a line-highlight treatment would be
 * designing for content that does not exist; both are noted here instead so they
 * are findable rather than forgotten. `language` IS emitted, as `data-language` —
 * it has no visual consumer yet either, but it is the hook syntax highlighting
 * would read, and an attribute makes the stored value inspectable in the markup.
 *
 * ── SYNTAX HIGHLIGHTING IS NOT HERE YET ──────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * Deliberately unhighlighted for now. Shiki with `createCssVariablesTheme` is the
 * likely route — it keeps the colors bound to tokens.css rather than importing a
 * VS Code palette, and runs at build time with no runtime JS — but the engine and
 * the loudness of the scheme are open decisions. This component is the baseline
 * either one layers onto: the change would be to the `<code>` contents, not to the
 * block's own rules.
 */
import type {Props as NodeProps} from 'astro-portabletext/types'

type CodeNode = {
  _type: 'code'
  _key?: string
  code?: string
  language?: string
  filename?: string
  highlightedLines?: number[]
}

type Props = NodeProps<CodeNode>

const {node} = Astro.props

/**
 * An empty block renders nothing rather than an empty tinted panel. The schema
 * makes `code` optional, so a block saved before anything was typed into it is a
 * real state, and a bare 60px-tall rectangle in the middle of an article is worse
 * than a gap.
 */
const code = node.code ?? ''

/**
 * Escaped by hand because `set:html` does not escape — that is what it is for.
 * Normally the answer would be `set:text`, which escapes for you, but that needs a
 * child element to sit on and a child element is the thing this component cannot
 * have (see above). So the escaping moves here.
 *
 * THREE REPLACEMENTS ARE THE COMPLETE SET for element content, and the order is
 * load-bearing. `&` must go first: doing `<` first would then re-escape the
 * ampersand in its own `&lt;` and render `&amp;lt;` on the page. Inside a `<code>`
 * element there is no attribute context and no comment context, so `"` and `'`
 * cannot break out and are deliberately left alone rather than escaped for
 * appearance's sake.
 *
 * This is the one place in the build where markup is assembled as a string, and it
 * is worth being explicit that CMS prose is not a trusted input just because the
 * CMS has one author.
 */
const html = `<code>${code
  .replace(/&/g, '&amp;')
  .replace(/</g, '&lt;')
  .replace(/>/g, '&gt;')}</code>`
---

{code && <pre tabindex="0" data-language={node.language ?? undefined} set:html={html} />}
